On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 02:06:27 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> jails/instances has config to build jails. Every time it does, it
> fetches all of the packages. I think it might be possible to have the
> packages saved at the host level, so they're cached across jail
> instances.

It is. The fix is posted as "[PATCH] jails: Cache packages on the
host across builds" [1].

Both installs in _jail.log.do keep their cache under the jail root:
pkg -c by its chroot, and pkg -r by the 188 base packages your output
shows it fetching on a fresh root. A clean build starts that root as
an empty dataset, so the cache is always empty. The patch keeps one
cache on the host at /jails/cache/pkg, which every instance shares.
It is nullfs-mounted onto the root's /var/cache/pkg around the two
pkg installs and unmounted afterwards, or by an EXIT trap if the
build fails, so the running jail never sees it. That fits
allow.mount.nonullfs, since the host does the mounting, not the jail.
The README says where the cache lives and how to empty it.

I could not test a real build here: lab-claude has no zroot/jails and
cannot mount. Two things to check on the host before you apply it:

- The build user needs doas rights to run mount -t nullfs and umount.
- Run a clean build twice. The second should print no "Fetching"
  lines, apart from the repo catalogs.

